{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1177 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "e3af5b30814f6156fb87ea8b92e0160e3e6fd57d0f3ac05f303e2f085d550a3e" :edb/db-before-t 1176 :edb/db-after-t 1177 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534489 50 #inst "2026-10-11T22:06:28.087Z" 13194139534489 17592186045417 true] [17592186198272 1038 #edb/ref 17592186422996 13194139534489 17592186045417 true] [17592186198273 1038 #edb/ref 17592186422995 13194139534489 17592186045417 true] [17592186198719 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,0.5]\\times [0,1]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,0.5],b\\in [0,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(0.5,0),$ $(0.5,1),$ and $(0,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5a50d17270e3856778a32461449d1e1abe36376be549e460a8fcee9ef6ea194b.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times [0,2i]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,i],b\\in [0,2i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(i,0),$ $(i,2i),$ and $(0,2i).$\n\n![](images/29/290a50d82323a459fbd0a790e28ff8889323cd5f08a0915f85b85a0aa0999490.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the point $(0,0):$\n\n![](images/ef/efb6d92ea0dc70fa0cbd9d82ea838b41a4eaab6ee2fc32c6fada9258a4154680.png)" 13194139534489 17592186045417 true] [17592186198719 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,0.5]\\times [0,1]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,0.5],b\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(0.5,0),$ $(0.5,1),$ and $(0,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5a50d17270e3856778a32461449d1e1abe36376be549e460a8fcee9ef6ea194b.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times [0,2i]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,i],b\\in [0,2i]}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(i,0),$ $(i,2i),$ and $(0,2i).$\n\n![](images/29/290a50d82323a459fbd0a790e28ff8889323cd5f08a0915f85b85a0aa0999490.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the point $(0,0):$\n\n![](images/ef/efb6d92ea0dc70fa0cbd9d82ea838b41a4eaab6ee2fc32c6fada9258a4154680.png)" 13194139534489 17592186045417 false] [17592186287753 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=N\\times R\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in N,\\,y\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/06/067b3b386273b09d5614f934f6525bd3e43ff46f420eb8aa7e744f635ff6d101.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}=\\{(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/a8/a870f65350ad00d258471079a34bec49a47d331c176dc7a91b3c7aae5ae968dd.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times N$ as the set of vertical lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/c0/c0df23ec47d729f597c02acff1530dd646a6f6c506a6497a8cb4d75b7fce5e74.png)" 13194139534489 17592186045417 true] [17592186287753 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=N\\times R\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in N,\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/06/067b3b386273b09d5614f934f6525bd3e43ff46f420eb8aa7e744f635ff6d101.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/a8/a870f65350ad00d258471079a34bec49a47d331c176dc7a91b3c7aae5ae968dd.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times N$ as the set of vertical lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/c0/c0df23ec47d729f597c02acff1530dd646a6f6c506a6497a8cb4d75b7fce5e74.png)" 13194139534489 17592186045417 false] [17592186367928 1071 "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [0,{i}^{2}].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139534489 17592186045417 true] [17592186367928 1071 "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,{i}^{2}].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139534489 17592186045417 false] [17592186367928 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=\\{1\\}\\times [0,{1}^{2}]=\\{(1,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/6b/6b6a47e464623fe9c572a52f7c71377136e68f41dcfd641920634b415568d7f5.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [0,{i}^{2}]=\\{(i,{k}^{2})\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,{i}^{2}).$ \n\n![](images/c6/c67cd0b545f0fc779d2977e38fab8164eb3b0161a18e32a615c71d88d1c30b68.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set." 13194139534489 17592186045417 true] [17592186367928 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}={1}\\times [0,{1}^{2}]={(1,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/6b/6b6a47e464623fe9c572a52f7c71377136e68f41dcfd641920634b415568d7f5.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,{i}^{2}]={(i,{k}^{2})\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,{i}^{2}).$ \n\n![](images/c6/c67cd0b545f0fc779d2977e38fab8164eb3b0161a18e32a615c71d88d1c30b68.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set." 13194139534489 17592186045417 false] [17592186389930 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS=R\\times [-1,1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in [-1,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}=\\{(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,1]\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/58/588f2574b1aaf0e96895816ca34bd9c6900a9b13342f44bd4104ad2bf4270bf5.png)\n\nNow, consider a general ${S}_{i}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some $i\\in R$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{i}=\\{(i,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,1]\\}\n$$\n\nFor each $i\\in R,$ ${S}_{i}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $(i,-1)$ to the point $(i,1).$\n\n![](images/84/8458978c2766d899b03149de53739650932875d6463c25dd3bbde99bc6a71ea7.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $R:$\n\n$$\nS={⋃}_{i\\in R}{S}_{i}.\n$$\n\nAs $i$ runs over $R,$ these line segments sweep the following bar that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in R}{S}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/6f/6fc81e487503ce86d3bf5366e57db087484e8bd1e8bafd52738c65e8e173c387.png)" 13194139534489 17592186045417 true] [17592186389930 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS=R\\times [-1,1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in [-1,1]}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}={(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,1]}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/58/588f2574b1aaf0e96895816ca34bd9c6900a9b13342f44bd4104ad2bf4270bf5.png)\n\nNow, consider a general ${S}_{i}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some $i\\in R$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{i}={(i,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,1]}\n$$\n\nFor each $i\\in R,$ ${S}_{i}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $(i,-1)$ to the point $(i,1).$\n\n![](images/84/8458978c2766d899b03149de53739650932875d6463c25dd3bbde99bc6a71ea7.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $R:$\n\n$$\nS={⋃}_{i\\in R}{S}_{i}.\n$$\n\nAs $i$ runs over $R,$ these line segments sweep the following bar that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in R}{S}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/6f/6fc81e487503ce86d3bf5366e57db087484e8bd1e8bafd52738c65e8e173c387.png)" 13194139534489 17592186045417 false] [17592186421830 1071 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=\\{-1,2,3\\}\\times (0,1)$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139534489 17592186045417 true] [17592186421830 1071 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={-1,2,3}\\times (0,1)$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139534489 17592186045417 false] [17592186421830 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=\\{-1,2,3\\}\\times (0,1)\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $-1,2,$ or $3$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\{-1,2,3\\},\\,y\\in (0,1)\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in (0,1)\\}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/1e/1e09e88b9f8bd472c1fd74b4d77a7aefff9b1170787899bc78fcf56e79518436.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}=\\{(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in (0,1)\\}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/3f/3f4ecac159eba9f49d6745ac2a46956fd19b68f0520974a6c618ebc1f118588a.png)\n\nNow, consider ${S}_{3}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $3$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1)].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}=\\{(3,y)\\,:\\,y\\in (0,1)\\}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/96/969ee218899817fe5bf497369fb6b2e5fa360fba0fbd4733c442c42208fa566c.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the three line segments shown below.\n\n![](images/7f/7f0ad1b126502e27710bcc97a72c4eec889a74f7f6dfac6449d98710ea16e16b.png)" 13194139534489 17592186045417 true] [17592186421830 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={-1,2,3}\\times (0,1)\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $-1,2,$ or $3$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x{-1,2,3},\\,y\\in (0,1)}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in (0,1)}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/1e/1e09e88b9f8bd472c1fd74b4d77a7aefff9b1170787899bc78fcf56e79518436.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in (0,1)}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/3f/3f4ecac159eba9f49d6745ac2a46956fd19b68f0520974a6c618ebc1f118588a.png)\n\nNow, consider ${S}_{3}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $3$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1)].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(3,y)\\,:\\,y\\in (0,1)}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/96/969ee218899817fe5bf497369fb6b2e5fa360fba0fbd4733c442c42208fa566c.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the three line segments shown below.\n\n![](images/7f/7f0ad1b126502e27710bcc97a72c4eec889a74f7f6dfac6449d98710ea16e16b.png)" 13194139534489 17592186045417 false] [17592186422983 1080 "images/9c/9c3083a4394093f53ff23f74f8b37e29d20da7cb693b2755fedfbe085b0ecf11.png" 13194139534489 17592186045417 true] [17592186422983 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 13194139534489 17592186045417 true] [17592186422983 1083 #uuid "0731ad6f-9b74-53f7-a4c5-a2106910f021" 13194139534489 17592186045417 true] [17592186422984 1080 "images/32/329932c9a6baaf874fd923dd2b87c8e0e3999fc66823d0e26d9f7307e62c55d0.png" 13194139534489 17592186045417 true] [17592186422984 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 13194139534489 17592186045417 true] [17592186422984 1083 #uuid "13c030cd-fe38-5f66-8922-566fca4fcde0" 13194139534489 